Title: THE REUNION TABLE
Author: Carmen Delgado
Draft date: June 2026

FADE IN:


EXT. MORALES HOME - ESTABLISHING - DAY

A full porch. Streamers. A reunion twenty years in the making.

INT. KITCHEN - DAY

MARIA commands the stove. ANTONIO chops, silent.
MARIA
Nobody fights at my table tonight.

INT. LIVING ROOM - DAY

Antonio's sister DIANA hangs photos. One frame face-down — their father.
DIANA
Mom still sets a plate for a ghost.

INT. GUEST BEDROOM - DAY

Antonio's wife packs a go-bag — ready to leave if it goes bad.
ANTONIO'S WIFE
Give her tonight. Then we decide.

INT. DINING ROOM - NIGHT

Twenty chairs. Wine poured. The doorbell.

EXT. FRONT PORCH - NIGHT

RAUL MORALES — older, tired, envelope in hand.
MARIA
You don't walk in like the years were a nap.

INT. DINING ROOM - NIGHT

Raul at the empty place. Children stare. Antonio stands.
RAUL
I came to tell you what the lawyers kept.

INT. KITCHEN - NIGHT

Antonio's cousin Diana reads the deed. Maria sinks onto a stool.
MARIA
He left the house to all of us.

EXT. BACK PORCH - NIGHT

Antonio and Raul alone. Crickets. Long pause.
RAUL
Twenty-five years of silence, and I only stopped lying about who owns what.

INT. DINING ROOM - NIGHT

Maria raises a glass around a table set for eighteen. Antonio does not join — yet. Diana reaches under the table, finds his hand, pulls him back to sit.
MARIA
Same roof. Same names. We eat first. We talk after.

FADE OUT.


THE END
